{"name":"Cliff’s View","hex":"#ddc5aa","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cliff","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vertical (or nearly vertical) rock face.","A point beyond which something abruptly fails or decreases in value, performance, etc."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Obsolete form of clef."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cliff"},{"term":"view","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Visual perception.\nThe act of seeing or looking at something.","The act of seeing or looking at something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To look at.","To regard in a stated way."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/view"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cliff","blocks":[{"text":"In geography and geology, a cliff or bluff or rock face is an area of rock which has a general angle defined by the vertical, or nearly vertical. Cliffs are formed by the processes of weathering and erosion, with the effect of gravity. Cliffs are common on coasts, in mountainous areas, escarpments and along rivers. Cliffs are usually composed of rock that is resistant to weathering and erosion. The sedimentary rocks that are most likely to form cliffs include sandstone, limestone, chalk, and dolomite. Igneous rocks such as granite and basalt also often form cliffs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG/330px-White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliff","wikidata":"Q107679"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Trango_Towers_2.jpg/500px-Trango_Towers_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Trango_Towers_2.jpg/500px-Trango_Towers_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Trango Towers in Pakistan. Their vertical faces are the world's tallest cliffs. Trango Tower center; Trango Monk center left; Trango II far left; Great Trango right.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrango_Towers_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg/500px-Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg/500px-Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Europe's highest cliff, Troll Wall in Norway, a famous BASE jumping location for jumpers from around the world.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATroll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Northern_Areas_40.jpg/500px-Northern_Areas_40.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Northern_Areas_40.jpg/1280px-Northern_Areas_40.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Northern_Areas_40.jpg/500px-Northern_Areas_40.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The far southwestern aspect of Nanga Parbat's Rupal face, highest cliff (rock wall/mountain face) in the world. The steepest part of the face is 2 km to the northeast. Cliffs are very common in areas where there are river banks and oceans.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANorthern_Areas_40.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Vihren_North_face.JPG/500px-Vihren_North_face.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Vihren_North_face.JPG/500px-Vihren_North_face.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.o